.TH "NOTMUCH-SHOW" "1" "Apr 17, 2026" "0.40" "notmuch"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
notmuch-show \- show messages matching the given search terms
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
.sp
\fBnotmuch\fP \fBshow\fP [\fIoption\fP ...] <\fIsearch\-term\fP> ...
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.sp
Shows all messages matching the search terms.
.sp
See notmuch\-search\-terms \%<#\:notmuch-search-terms-7> for details of the supported syntax for
<search\-terms>.
.sp
The messages will be grouped and sorted based on the threading (all
replies to a particular message will appear immediately after that
message in date order). The output is not indented by default, but depth
tags are printed so that proper indentation can be performed by a
post\-processor (such as the emacs interface to notmuch).
.sp
Supported options for \fBshow\fP include
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-duplicate=N
Output duplicate number N. The numbering starts from 1, and matches
the order used by \fBsearch \-\-duplicate\fP \%<#\:cmdoption-search-duplicate> and
\fBsearch \-\-output=files\fP \%<#\:cmdoption-search-output>
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-entire\-thread=(true|false)
If true, \fBnotmuch show\fP outputs all messages in the thread of
any message matching the search terms; if false, it outputs only
the matching messages. For \fB\-\-format=json\fP and \fB\-\-format=sexp\fP
this defaults to true. For other formats, this defaults to false.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-format=(text|json|sexp|mbox|raw)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B text (default for messages)
The default plain\-text format has all text\-content MIME parts
decoded. Various components in the output, (\fBmessage\fP,
\fBheader\fP, \fBbody\fP, \fBattachment\fP, and MIME \fBpart\fP), will
be delimited by easily\-parsed markers. Each marker consists of
a Control\-L character (ASCII decimal 12), the name of the
marker, and then either an opening or closing brace, (\(aq{\(aq or
\(aq}\(aq), to either open or close the component. For a multipart
MIME message, these parts will be nested.
.TP
.B json
The output is formatted with Javascript Object Notation
(JSON). This format is more robust than the text format for
automated processing. The nested structure of multipart MIME
messages is reflected in nested JSON output. By default JSON
output includes all messages in a matching thread; that is, by
default, \fB\-\-format=json\fP sets \fB\-\-entire\-thread\fP\&. The
caller can disable this behaviour by setting
\fB\-\-entire\-thread=false\fP\&.  The JSON output is always encoded
as UTF\-8 and any message content included in the output will
be charset\-converted to UTF\-8.
.TP
.B sexp
The output is formatted as the Lisp s\-expression (sexp)
equivalent of the JSON format above. Objects are formatted as
property lists whose keys are keywords (symbols preceded by a
colon). True is formatted as \fBt\fP and both false and null are
formatted as \fBnil\fP\&. As for JSON, the s\-expression output is
always encoded as UTF\-8.
.TP
.B mbox
All matching messages are output in the traditional, Unix mbox
format with each message being prefixed by a line beginning
with \(dqFrom \(dq and a blank line separating each message. Lines
in the message content beginning with \(dqFrom \(dq (preceded by
zero or more \(aq>\(aq characters) have an additional \(aq>\(aq character
added. This reversible escaping is termed \(dqmboxrd\(dq format and
described in detail here:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
\%<http://\:homepage\:.ntlworld\:.com/\:jonathan\:.deboynepollard/\:FGA/\:mail-mbox-formats\:.html>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B raw (default if \fB\-\-part\fP is given)
Write the raw bytes of the given MIME part of a message to
standard out. For this format, it is an error to specify a
query that matches more than one message.
.sp
If the specified part is a leaf part, this outputs the body of
the part after performing content transfer decoding (but no
charset conversion). This is suitable for saving attachments,
for example.
.sp
For a multipart or message part, the output includes the part
headers as well as the body (including all child parts). No
decoding is performed because multipart and message parts
cannot have non\-trivial content transfer encoding. Consumers
of this may need to implement MIME decoding and similar
functions.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-format\-version=N
Use the specified structured output format version. This is
intended for programs that invoke notmuch \%<#\:notmuch-1> internally. If
omitted, the latest supported version will be used.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-part=N
Output the single decoded MIME part N of a single message. The
search terms must match only a single message. Message parts are
numbered in a depth\-first walk of the message MIME structure, and
are identified in the \(aqjson\(aq, \(aqsexp\(aq or \(aqtext\(aq output formats.
.sp
Note that even a message with no MIME structure or a single body
part still has two MIME parts: part 0 is the whole message
(headers and body) and part 1 is just the body.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-sort=(newest\-first|oldest\-first)
This option can be used to present results in either chronological
order (\fBoldest\-first\fP) or reverse chronological order
(\fBnewest\-first\fP).
.sp
Only threads as a whole are reordered.  Ordering of messages within
each thread will not be affected by this flag, since that order is
always determined by the thread\(aqs replies.
.sp
By default, results will be displayed in reverse chronological
order, (that is, the newest results will be displayed first).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-offset=[\-]N
Skip displaying the first N results. With the leading \(aq\-\(aq, start
at the Nth result from the end.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-limit=N
Limit the number of displayed results to N.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Compute and report the validity of any MIME cryptographic
signatures found in the selected content (e.g., \(dqmultipart/signed\(dq
parts). Status of the signature will be reported (currently only
supported with \fB\-\-format=json\fP and \fB\-\-format=sexp\fP), and the
multipart/signed part will be replaced by the signed data.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-decrypt=(false|auto|true|stash)
If \fBtrue\fP, decrypt any MIME encrypted parts found in the
selected content (e.g., \(dqmultipart/encrypted\(dq parts). Status of
the decryption will be reported (currently only supported
with \fB\-\-format=json\fP and \fB\-\-format=sexp\fP) and on successful
decryption the multipart/encrypted part will be replaced by
the decrypted content.
.sp
\fBstash\fP behaves like \fBtrue\fP, but upon successful decryption it
will also stash the message\(aqs session key in the database, and
index the cleartext of the message, enabling automatic decryption
in the future.
.sp
If \fBauto\fP, and a session key is already known for the
message, then it will be decrypted, but notmuch will not try
to access the user\(aqs keys.
.sp
Use \fBfalse\fP to avoid even automatic decryption.
.sp
Non\-automatic decryption (\fBstash\fP or \fBtrue\fP, in the absence of
a stashed session key) expects a functioning \fBgpg\-agent(1) \%<https://\:manpages\:.debian\:.org/\:gpg-agent\:.1\:.html>\fP to
provide any needed credentials. Without one, the decryption will
fail.
.sp
Note: setting either \fBtrue\fP or \fBstash\fP here implies
\fB\-\-verify\fP\&.
.sp
Here is a table that summarizes each of these policies:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l|l|l.
T{
